/**

\page tarch_plotting Plotting

All plotting in Peano is typically realised through the files in tarch/plotter.
The idea is that you have an action set which dumps the data. To do so, it owns
a plotter class. This implies that each tree in the system owns its own plotter
and writes (logically) into its own output file. The plotter under the hood
might do something clever with merging those guys, but logically it is a one
file per action set approach.


## Action set pattern

You never know how much meta data a plotter object holds and when this data is
dumped into the file. Therefore, most action sets which dump data create the
plotter object within beginTraversal(). They release this object again in
endTraversal(), which is the latest point when the plotter will actually stream
its data into a file. In modern code the action set holds the writer in a
std::unique_ptr, creates it through std::make_unique() and releases it via
reset(), so there are no raw new/delete calls and the writer is destroyed (and
hence flushed) deterministically.


## File writers and time series

The plotting is split into two responsibilities. The actual data of one
traversal is written through a concrete subclass of the abstract
tarch::plotter::FileWriter. Peano ships VTU (tarch::plotter::VTUFileWriter),
HDF5 (tarch::plotter::HDF5FileWriter) and NetCDF
(tarch::plotter::NetCDFFileWriter) implementations. A file writer is told an
output directory and the number of local subdomains (trees) and then hands out
vertex, cell and data plotters via its create...() factory methods.

The bookkeeping over all snapshots, i.e. the index/meta file that ties the
individual snapshots into one time series, is handled separately by a subclass
of tarch::plotter::TimeSeriesWriter. The VTU writer pairs with
tarch::plotter::PVDTimeSeriesWriter (a ParaView .pvd index), HDF5/NetCDF pair
with tarch::plotter::XDMFTimeSeriesWriter, and
tarch::plotter::JSONTimeSeriesWriter is available too.

A plotting action set typically owns both writers as std::unique_ptr. The time
series writer is created once and kept alive for the whole simulation, while
the file writer is recreated per snapshot so that it picks up the current
number of subdomains (which may change under dynamic load balancing):

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  if (_timeSeriesWriter == nullptr) {
    _timeSeriesWriter = std::make_unique<tarch::plotter::PVDTimeSeriesWriter>();
  }

  _fileWriter.reset();
  _fileWriter = std::make_unique<tarch::plotter::VTUFileWriter>(
    runner::RuntimeParameters::getInstance().getOutputPath(),
    numberOfLocalSubdomains
  );
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Once a traversal has filled the file writer, the snapshot is committed and
registered with the time series in one go:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  _timeSeriesWriter->writeSnapshotFile(filename, time, *_fileWriter);
  _timeSeriesWriter->writeTimeSeriesMetaFile(
    runner::RuntimeParameters::getInstance().getOutputPath(), filename
  );
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

writeSnapshotFile() delegates to the file writer to dump the actual snapshot
and remembers the (time, filename) pair, and writeTimeSeriesMetaFile() rewrites
the index/meta file so that the new snapshot becomes visible to the
post-processing tools.


## Parallel plotting

Writing the shared time series meta file is neither thread-safe nor MPI-safe.
That is, you should not update it concurrently from two threads or two ranks.
To ensure that this does not happen, you typically wrap the critical region
into a tarch::multicore::Lock around a global instance of
tarch::multicore::BooleanSemaphore.


*/